	<key>readme</key>
	<string>## Setup

The workflow reads the database which the Evernote app keeps on your Mac. When macOS asks if Alfred may access data from other apps, choose Allow. If the workflow reports that it is not allowed to read Evernote’s data, grant Alfred Full Disk Access in System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security.

## Usage

Search your Evernote notes via the `ens` keyword. It looks in titles, note text, tags, notebook names, and the text of attachments.

* &lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; Open note in Evernote.
* &lt;kbd&gt;⌘&lt;/kbd&gt;&lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; Open note on evernote.com.
* &lt;kbd&gt;⌥&lt;/kbd&gt;&lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; Copy link to note.

Search note titles only via the `ent` keyword.

Type several words to narrow the results. Put a star in front of a word to find it inside longer words: `*port` finds `airport`. Use either keyword without words to list the notes changed most recently.

Searching attachments and matching every word inside longer words can be turned off in the Workflow’s Configuration. If the Mac holds more than one Evernote account, choose the database file there as well; otherwise the most recently modified database is searched.

Up to 40 notes are shown and the first eight words are used. Matching inside longer words covers titles and note text.

Notes are read from Evernote’s local database, so no API key or network access is needed and only notes synced to this Mac are found. The database is opened read-only and is never locked, so Evernote is not held up. In return, a search made at the moment Evernote saves can show incomplete results; the workflow then searches again and says so if Evernote is still saving. Evernote does not document the database, so an Evernote update may require a workflow update.

Requires Alfred 5. Tested with Evernote 11.35 from the Mac App Store. This workflow is not affiliated with or endorsed by Evernote.
</string>
